docs/guide/audio.md:499:When two clips on one track overlap with a transition, the transition can also crossfade their sound. Turn on **Crossfade Audio** in the clip's Transition section. The crossfade keeps the combined loudness steady across the overlap. See [Motion and transitions](motion-and-transitions.md).
docs/guide/motion-and-transitions.md:363:| Crossfade Audio | On: the outgoing sound fades out and the incoming fades in, eased by the Curve, in the Crossfade Shape. A pair where only one clip has sound still fades that clip. Off: both clips play at their own level across the whole overlap, mixed together. Mute, solo, ducking and volume still apply on top | On or off |
docs/guide/motion-and-transitions.md:364:| Crossfade Shape | The curve of the crossfade, enabled while Crossfade Audio is on. **+3 dB** (constant power, the default) holds the level steady between two unrelated takes. **Linear** is a straight-line pair, which dips 3 dB in the middle of unrelated takes but suits two parts of the same sound. **S-Curve** and **−3 dB** move more of the change to the middle or the end. The incoming clip follows the shape and the outgoing clip follows it in reverse | +3 dB, Linear, S-Curve, −3 dB |
